\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Game Theory: Homework 4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
There are two players, a seller and a buyer.
 At date 1, the seller chooses his irreversible investment level 
\begin_inset Formula $I\geq0$
\end_inset

 at cost 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

.
 At date 2, the seller may sell one unit of a good to the buyer, and the
 cost of supplying the good is 
\begin_inset Formula $c(I)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{\prime}(I)<0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{I\rightarrow\infty}c^{\prime}(I)=-\infty$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{\prime\prime}>0$
\end_inset

.
 To simplify, assume 
\begin_inset Formula $c(0)$
\end_inset

 is less than the buyer's valuation for the good, 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

.
 There is no discounting.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the socially optimal level of investment?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that at date 2 the buyer observes the investment made by the seller
 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 and makes him a take-it-or-leave-it offer to buy the good for 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 What is this offer? What is the subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium of the
 game?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Can you think of a contractual way of avoiding the inefficient outcome of
 part b? (Assume that contracts cannot be written on the level of 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Define the indicator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if good sold}\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(I,q)=pq-I-c(I)q$
\end_inset

 be the seller's profit function and 
\begin_inset Formula $CS=vq-pq$
\end_inset

 be the buyer's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

surplus.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 To find the socially optimal level of investment, the social planner wishes
 to maximize the total surplus function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{I}}\ TS(I,q)=\pi+CS=p\cdot q-I-c(I)\cdot q+v\cdot q-p\cdot q=v\cdot q-c(I)\cdot q-I
\]

\end_inset

Taking the derivative of the objective function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial TS}{\partial q}=v-c(I)>0
\]

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $v-c(0)>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c(I)$
\end_inset

 is decreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

.
 Define the function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q^{*}(p)=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if\;}v\geq p\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}(p)=0\implies TS(I,0)=-I\leq0$
\end_inset


\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}(p)=1\implies TS(I,1)=v-c(I)-I$
\end_inset

.
 
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
To achieve the socially optimal level of investment, let 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}(p)=1$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $I=0$
\end_inset

 is always possible, and 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $TS(I,1)>0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $I=0$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order condition of 
\begin_inset Formula $TS$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c^{\prime}(I)=-1
\]

\end_inset

This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $I^{*}=c^{\prime-1}(-1)$
\end_inset

 is the socially optimal level of investment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To determine the offer made by the buyer, as well as the subgame-perfect
 Nash equilibrium, we will solve the came using backward induction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Third Stage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The seller maximizes his profit by playing a best response to the given
 price level and optimally choosing whether or not to sell the good.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi^{*}(q)=\begin{cases}
p-I-c(I) & \mbox{if}\; q=1\\
-I & \mbox{if}\; q=0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q^{*}(p)=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\; p\geq c(I)\\
0 & \mbox{if}\; p<c(I)
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
Second Stage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The buyer selects a price for the good to maximize his 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

consumer surplus
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 given the best response function of the seller in the third stage.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{p}}\ CS(p)=v\cdot q^{*}(p)-p\cdot q^{*}(p)
\]

\end_inset

There are two cases to consider:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p<c(I)\implies CS(p)=0
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\geq c(I)\implies CS(p)=v-p
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\; I:v>c(0)>c(I)$
\end_inset

, then the buyer will maximize his consumer surplus by setting 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}(I)=c(I)\implies\forall\; I:CS(p^{*}(I))>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
First stage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given the best response functions of the previous stages, the seller maximizes
 his profit by optimally selecting a level of investment.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{I\geq0}}\ \pi(I)=p^{*}(I)\cdot q^{*}(p^{*}(I))-I-c(I)\cdot q^{*}(p^{*}(I))
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}(I)=c(I)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(I)=-I$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $I^{*}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, the subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
SPNE:\;(I^{*}=0,\ q^{*}=1;\; p^{*}(0)=c(0))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To avoid the inefficient outcome of part (b), both the buyer and seller
 must agree to a price level in the first stage.
 To achieve the socially optimal level of investment, i.e., 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $I^{*}=c^{\prime-1}(-1)$
\end_inset

, set 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}=c(c^{\prime-1}(-1))+c^{\prime-1}(-1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the seller to agree to sell the good, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(I)=p-c(I)-I\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi(I)=c(c^{\prime-1}(-1))+c^{\prime-1}(-1)-c(c^{\prime-1}(-1))-c^{\prime-1}(-1)=0
\]

\end_inset

So, the seller is indifferent between producing and not producing the good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, for the buyer to agree to this price, 
\begin_inset Formula $CS(p^{*})>CS(c(0))$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v-(c(c^{\prime-1}(-1))+c^{\prime-1}(-1))>v-c(0)
\]

\end_inset

which implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c(c^{\prime-1}(-1))+c^{\prime-1}(-1)<c(0)
\]

\end_inset

It is important to note that, 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\forall\; I>0:TS(I,1)>TS(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\; I>0:v-c(I)-I\geq v-c(0)
\]

\end_inset

which implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\; I>0:c(I)+I<c(0)
\]

\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
Therefore, the buyer will agree to the new price since it strictly improves
 his 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

surplus.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the following two stage model discussed in class.
 In the first stage, firms simultaneously choose whether to enter the market,
 resulting in a one time sunk cost of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 for each entrant.
 In the second stage, the firms that chose to enter compete Cournot.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that there are 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 identical consumers with individual demand functions 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=\alpha-\beta p$
\end_inset

.
 Each firm has a constant marginal cost of production 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
 Derive the Cournot price and quantity as a function of the number of firms
 on the market 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 and the number of consumers 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Ignoring the integer problem, find the number of firms on the market that
 will choose to enter in the first stage in an SPNE of this game.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What happens to the equilibrium number of entrants, the equilibrium price
 and quantity as 
\begin_inset Formula $I\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

? Explain.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Endogenous entry: entry will occur as long as set-up costs 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 are less than potential profits.
 The totally quantity demanded is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q(p)={\displaystyle \sum_{i=01}^{I}}(\alpha-\beta p)=I(\alpha-\beta p)
\]

\end_inset

So, the inverse demand function is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(Q)=\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{Q}{I\beta}
\]

\end_inset

Constant 
\begin_inset Formula $MC=c>0$
\end_inset

.
 Timing: All potential firms simultaneously decide in or out where in is
 associated with a set-up cost 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 All entrants 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 then play a Cournot came.
 We solve the game by backward induction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Stage 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 firms have entered the market.
 Firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

's optimization problem under Cournot is as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{q_{j}}}\left(p(Q)-c\right)\cdot q_{j}=\left[\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{1}{I\beta}{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}q_{k}}\right)-c\right]\cdot q_{j}
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{1}{I\beta}\left(2q_{j}+{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}^{J}}q_{k}\right)-c=0
\]

\end_inset

So, the best response function for firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{j}(q_{-j})=\dfrac{IB}{2}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{1}{I\beta}{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq j}^{J}}q_{k}-c\right)
\]

\end_inset

We assume that we have a symmetric NE, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\; j\neq k:q_{j}^{*}=q_{k}^{*}=q^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{j}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
q_{j}^{*} & = & \dfrac{IB}{2}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{(J-1)}{I\beta}q_{j}^{*}-c\right)\\
q_{j}^{*}+\dfrac{(J-1)}{2}q_{j}^{*} & = & \dfrac{I\beta}{2}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)\\
\dfrac{2q_{j}^{*}}{2}+\dfrac{Jq_{j}^{*}}{2}-\dfrac{q_{j}^{*}}{2} & = & \dfrac{I\beta}{2}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)\\
\left(J+1\right)q_{j}^{*} & = & I\beta\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)\\
q_{j}^{*} & = & \dfrac{I\beta}{\left(J+1\right)}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)\\
 & = & \dfrac{I\alpha}{\left(J+1\right)}-\dfrac{I\beta c}{\left(J+1\right)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, we can derive the Cournot price and quantity
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Q^{*}=\sum_{j=1}^{J}q_{j}^{*} & = & \dfrac{JI\beta}{(J+1)}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)=\frac{JI\left(\alpha-\beta c\right)}{(J+1)}\\
p^{*}(Q^{*}) & = & \dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)=\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}+\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}c
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Ignoring the integer problem, we find the number of firms on the market
 that will choose to enter in the first stage in an SPNE of this game.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\pi_{j}^{*} & = & p(Q^{*})\cdot q_{j}^{*}-c\cdot q_{j}^{*}\\
 & = & \left[\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)\right]\left[\dfrac{I\beta}{\left(J+1\right)}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)\right]-c\left[\dfrac{I\beta}{\left(J+1\right)}\left(\dfrac{\alpha}{\beta}-c\right)\right]\\
 & = & \dfrac{I}{(J+1)}\dfrac{\alpha^{2}}{\beta}-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\dfrac{I}{(J+1)}\dfrac{\alpha^{2}}{\beta}+\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\dfrac{2I}{(J+1)}\alpha c\\
 &  & -\dfrac{2I}{(J+1)}\alpha c+\dfrac{I\beta}{(J+1)}c^{2}-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\dfrac{I\beta}{(J+1)}c^{2}\\
 & = & \left[1-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\right]\dfrac{I}{(J+1)}\dfrac{\alpha^{2}}{\beta}+\left[\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}-1\right]\dfrac{2I}{(J+1)}\alpha c+\left[1-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\right]\dfrac{I\beta}{(J+1)}c^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
Stage 1: Entry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $J^{*}$
\end_inset

 be the the SPNE entrants in the market.
 No deviation incentives requires: 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{*}(J^{*})\geq K$
\end_inset

 and 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{*}(J^{*}+1)<K$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
.
 Firm 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 is exactly indifferent between entry and staying out if 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{J}^{*}=K$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
K & = & \left[1-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\right]\dfrac{I}{(J+1)}\dfrac{\alpha^{2}}{\beta}\\
 &  & +\left[\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}-1\right]\dfrac{2I}{(J+1)}\alpha c+\left[1-\dfrac{J}{(J+1)}\right]\dfrac{I\beta}{(J+1)}c^{2}\\
K(J+1)^{2} & = & \left[(J+1)-J\right]I\dfrac{\alpha^{2}}{\beta}\\
 &  & +\left[J-(J+1)\right]2I\alpha c+\left[(J+1)-J\right]I\beta c^{2}\\
K(J+1)^{2} & = & \dfrac{I\alpha^{2}}{\beta}-2Ic\alpha+I\beta c^{2}\\
J^{*} & = & \sqrt{\dfrac{I\alpha^{2}}{K\beta}-\dfrac{2Ic\alpha}{K}+\dfrac{I\beta c^{2}}{K}}-1\\
 & = & \left(a-c\beta\right)\sqrt{\dfrac{I}{K\beta}}-1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What happens to the equilibrium number of entrants, the equilibrium price
 and quantity as 
\begin_inset Formula $I\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

?
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
J^{*}=\left(a-c\beta\right)\sqrt{\dfrac{I}{K\beta}}-1
\]

\end_inset

As 
\begin_inset Formula $I\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, the equilibrium number of entrants approaches infinity, i.e., 
