\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Does either player have a strictly dominant strategy in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

? If so, what is it?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find all Nash equilibria (mixed and pure) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Now, consider the super game 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma^{\infty}(\delta)$
\end_inset

, which consists of repeatedly playing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

 forever and in which the players possess the common discount factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that the players wish to support a SPNE in which they play 
\begin_inset Formula $(Y_{1},Z_{2})$
\end_inset

 in even period 
\begin_inset Formula $(t=0,2,4,\ldots)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(Z_{1},Y_{2})$
\end_inset

 in odd periods 
\begin_inset Formula $(t=1,3,5,\ldots)$
\end_inset

.
 What is the discounted date-zero payoff to the players on the path suggested
 above?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the value 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{\delta}$
\end_inset

 such that the path suggested above can be supported in SPNE if and only
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\geq\underline{\delta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{1}$
\end_inset

 strictly dominates 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{2}$
\end_inset

 strictly dominates 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By performing iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies, we
 achieve the unique pure strategy Nash equilibrium: 
\begin_inset Formula $(Z_{1},Z_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 There is not a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium because neither player would
 put positive probability on their strictly dominated strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now consider the supergame 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma^{\infty}(\delta)$
\end_inset

, which consists of repeatedly playing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

 forever and in which the players possess the common discount factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\;\epsilon\;(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that the players wish to support a SPNE in which they play 
\begin_inset Formula $(Y_{1},Z_{2})$
\end_inset

 in even periods 
\begin_inset Formula $(t=0,2,4,\ldots)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(Z_{1},Y_{2})$
\end_inset

 in odd periods 
\begin_inset Formula $(t=1,3,5,\ldots)$
\end_inset

.
 What is the discounted date-zero payoff to the players on the path suggested
 above?
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{1} & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}(-1)\delta^{2t}+2\delta}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\delta^{2t}\\
 & = & \left(2\delta-1\right){\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\delta^{2t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{2} & = & 2{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\delta^{2t}-\delta{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\delta^{2t}\\
 & = & (2-\delta){\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\delta^{2t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Find the value 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{\delta}$
\end_inset

 such that the path suggested above can be supported in SPNE if and only
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\geq\underline{\delta}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
u_{1}=\dfrac{2\delta-1}{1-\delta^{2}}\geq0\implies\delta\geq\dfrac{1}{2}\\
u_{2}=\dfrac{2-\delta}{1-\delta^{2}}\geq0\implies2\geq\delta\\
\underline{\delta}=\dfrac{1}{2}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Suppose that there are two identical firms serving the market with inverse
 demand of
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p=1-Q
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 denotes the market output and 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 denotes the market price.
 Each firm's cost function is 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}(q_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Consider a quantity-setting supergame.
 Calculate the lowest discount parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{\delta}_{C}$
\end_inset

 such that collusion on the monopoly output, in which the two firms share
 the earnings evenly can be supported in a SPNE with a trigger strategy
 of Cournot-Nash reversion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Consider a symmetric price setting supergame.
 Calculate the lowest discount parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{\delta}_{B}$
\end_inset

 such that collusion on the monopoly price, in which the two firms share
 the earnings evenly can be supported in a SPNE with a trigger strategy
 of Bertrand-Nash reversion.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The monopoly problem can be given as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{Q}}\ (1-Q)Q
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1-2Q & = & 0\\
Q^{m} & = & \frac{1}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p^{m} & = & \frac{1}{2}\\
q_{i}^{m} & = & \frac{1}{4}\\
\pi_{i}^{m} & = & \frac{1}{8}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The Cournot-Nash problem for firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 can be written as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{q_{i}}}\ (1-q_{i}-q_{j})q_{i}
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1-2q_{i}-q_{j} & = & 0\\
q_{i}^{c} & = & \frac{1-q_{j}^{c}}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
q_{i}^{c} & = & \frac{1}{2}-\frac{1-q_{i}^{c}}{4}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{4}+\frac{q_{i}^{c}}{4}\\
q_{i}^{c} & = & \frac{1}{3}\\
p_{i}^{c} & = & \frac{1}{3}\\
\pi_{i}^{c} & = & \frac{1}{9}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

If one of the firms chooses to deviate, then its optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 would be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1-\nicefrac{1}{4}}{2}=\frac{3}{8}
\]

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\nicefrac{5}{8}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\nicefrac{3}{8}$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}=\nicefrac{9}{64}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{j}=\nicefrac{3}{32}$
\end_inset

.
 If both firms collude, then each firm's discounted payoff is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EU_{i}=\frac{1}{8}(1+\delta+\delta^{2}+\cdots)=\frac{1}{8(1-\delta)}
\]

\end_inset

Firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's payoff from deivating at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
EU_{i} & = & \frac{9}{64}+\frac{1}{9}(\delta+\delta^{2}+\cdots)\\
 & = & \frac{9}{64}+\frac{\delta}{9(1-\delta)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In order for collusion to be supported, we require:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{8(1-\delta)} & \geq & \frac{9}{64}+\frac{\delta}{9(1-\delta)}\\
1 & \geq & \frac{9}{8}-\frac{9\delta}{8}+\frac{8\delta}{9}\\
\delta & \geq & \frac{9}{17}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, the lowest discount factor for which collusion can be supported is 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{\delta}_{C}=\nicefrac{9}{17}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The monopoly problem can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{p}}\ p(1-p)
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1-2p & = & 0\\
p^{m} & = & \frac{1}{2}\\
Q^{m} & = & \frac{1}{2}\\
q_{i}^{m} & = & \frac{1}{2}\\
\pi_{i}^{m} & = & \frac{1}{8}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

If one of the firm's deviates from the monopoly price level, then that firm
 would get the entire profit share.
 So, the firm deviatting from collusion would choose 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

 slightly less than 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

, and thus, their profit will be slightly less than 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

.
 For the Bertrand-Nash problem, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}^{b}=p_{j}^{b}=0$
\end_inset

 because each firm has a cost of zero.
 So, in this case, profit for each firm will also be zero.
 Thus, the payoff of firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from collusion will be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
EU_{i} & = & \frac{1}{8}(1+\delta+\delta^{2}+\cdots)\\
EU_{i} & = & \frac{1}{8(1-\delta)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Deviating from collusion in the first period would yield
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EU_{i}<\frac{1}{4}
\]

\end_inset

In order for collusion to be supported, we should have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{8(1-\delta)} & \geq & \frac{1}{4}\\
\delta & \geq & \frac{1}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Therefore, the lowest discount parameter to support collusion is 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{\delta}_{B}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

